WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
This is The Spread, your energy
markets briefing for Monday,

2
00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.680
the twenty-sixth of October.

3
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.560
This episode is written with AI,
and read by an AI clone of my voice.

4
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.320
Brent closed last week above one hundred
and sixteen dollars after further

5
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.720
attacks on Saudi Arabia. That came
even though Middle East crude exports

6
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.320
are now running above pre-war levels.

7
00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.600
Freight is the other squeeze: tanker
costs are pricing US crude out of

8
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.307
Asia and pushing fuel oil
above crude in Singapore.

9
00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:33.040
On the markets. Brent topped one
hundred and sixteen dollars a barrel

10
00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.360
on Friday, according to Rigzone, and
OilPrice dot com had it above one

11
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.960
hundred and seventeen by Friday evening.

12
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.440
Goldman Sachs puts the global average
crude freight rate above twenty

13
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:46.680
dollars a barrel, against
three dollars a year ago.

14
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.840
EU gas storage stood at seventy-four point
six percent full on the twenty-fourth

15
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:54.320
of October, according to
Gas Infrastructure Europe.

16
00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.800
Starting in the Gulf. Friday's
rally built through the day,

17
00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.600
with Brent touching one hundred and
fourteen dollars intraday that morning

18
00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.320
before pushing higher as the
attacks on Saudi Arabia escalated,

19
00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.560
Rigzone reported. What makes it
unusual is the supply picture.

20
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.760
According to Kpler, October Middle
East oil exports ran higher than before

21
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.467
the US and Israeli strikes on
Iran at the end of February.

22
00:01:17.493 --> 00:01:20.160
The physical market
isn't following futures.

23
00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.920
Prices for some Middle East crudes
sold outside the Gulf actually fell

24
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.240
on Friday. So the premium in Brent
reflects the risk of losing Saudi

25
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.800
barrels, not a shortage today.

26
00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:34.520
Refiners buying on futures-linked
formulas pay that premium anyway,

27
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.360
even with cargoes available.

28
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.600
What's still unclear is the damage.

29
00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:42.133
The reports we've seen today don't
say which Saudi sites were hit,

30
00:01:42.187 --> 00:01:44.040
or whether output was affected.

31
00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.000
A statement from Aramco or the Saudi
energy ministry would settle it.

32
00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:52.000
Also in the region, Ras Al Khaimah
closed all its beaches on Saturday

33
00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.520
after an offshore oil leak the day before,

34
00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.160
Reuters reported. The source
of the leak hasn't been given.

35
00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:02.280
Now to shipping, where freight is
cutting US crude off from Asia.

36
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.240
Earlier this month a voyage from the
US Gulf Coast to China cost as much

37
00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.960
as eighty million dollars,
according to OilPrice dot com.

38
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.760
That's about ten times the pre-war norm.

39
00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.200
It has since eased, but only
to around sixty million.

40
00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:19.920
At those rates there's little
reason to send US crude east.

41
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.320
gCaptain reports that US crude exports
to Asia are on course for multi-year

42
00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.400
lows, possibly the weakest in seven years.

43
00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.200
More light sweet crude stays in
the Atlantic Basin as a result,

44
00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.400
and Asian refiners lean
on supply closer to home.

45
00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.720
The cost is feeding into products too.

46
00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:40.960
Singapore high-sulphur fuel oil now
trades above the crude it's made

47
00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.880
from, OilPrice dot com reported.

48
00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.320
Behind that are lost Middle
East and Russian supply,

49
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.840
steady bunker demand and the cost
of shipping replacement cargoes.

50
00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.600
Very-low-sulphur fuel oil
may ease towards year-end,

51
00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:57.520
if more Gulf supply and extra
Chinese export quotas arrive.

52
00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.840
Turning to diesel. The Russian
cargo bound for the US has sailed,

53
00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.960
and is expected in Norfolk,
Virginia, early next month,

54
00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.760
according to gCaptain. A second
cargo is heading for New York.

55
00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.400
It would be the first Russian diesel
to reach the US in more than three

56
00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:15.160
years, though volumes
haven't been reported.

57
00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.160
Europe's diesel and jet
supply remains tight.

58
00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.360
The European Commission says
there's no physical shortage,

59
00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:25.280
according to Rigzone. Germany says
it will try to stop further refinery

60
00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.920
closures, but hasn't
detailed any measures.

61
00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.280
On gas, that storage figure was down
a hundredth of a point on the day.

62
00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.920
More to the point, it's the same
level as on the eighteenth of October.

63
00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.040
Net injections have been
flat for about a week,

64
00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:40.920
just as Europe heads into winter.

65
00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.560
Britain is expected to lean more
heavily on imports this winter,

66
00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:48.640
Rigzone reported. Its proven and
probable reserves fell to two point

67
00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:52.080
five billion barrels of oil
equivalent at the end of last year,

68
00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.800
according to the North
Sea Transition Authority.

69
00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.440
That's a drop of zero point four billion.

70
00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:00.560
No exploration wells were
drilled in that year at all.

71
00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.640
Back in the US, the Department of
Energy on Friday authorised an emergency

72
00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.600
exchange from the Strategic
Petroleum Reserve.

73
00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.960
Up to four hundred thousand barrels
will move from the West Hackberry

74
00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.560
site to the Phillips sixty-six
refinery at Lake Charles.

75
00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:18.320
The department cited a temporary
disruption to crude deliveries and gave

76
00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:22.960
no cause. The reserve is already at
its lowest since nineteen eighty-two,

77
00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:27.200
OilPrice dot com noted, and this release
keeps one Gulf Coast plant running

78
00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.280
rather than adding product supply.

79
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:33.760
And in the Black Sea, a drone strike
sank a cargo ship off Romania on

80
00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.600
Friday, Reuters reported.

81
00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.400
One person was killed. Three are missing.

82
00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.680
Romania's president blames Russia.

83
00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:44.880
Separately, Russia's defence ministry
says its drones hit a cargo vessel

84
00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.720
bound for Chornomorsk and a
second ship overnight into Sunday,

85
00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.440
which Kyiv hasn't confirmed
in today's reports.

86
00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:55.680
A few more things. Hurricane Isaias
shut-in figures from the Bureau of

87
00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:59.200
Safety and Environmental
Enforcement are still unpublished,

88
00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.440
overdue since the sixteenth of October.

89
00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:05.440
And the Office of the US Trade
Representative has proposed keeping the

90
00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.840
suspension of port fees on China-linked
ships in place into January next

91
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:10.640
year.

92
00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:14.960
What to watch next. On Wednesday,
the EIA's weekly figures,

93
00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.880
covering the week to the twenty-third.

94
00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:21.200
On Sunday, the first of November,
TotalEnergies' diesel and heating-oil

95
00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.200
price cuts in France take effect,
alongside a release from French state

96
00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:29.800
reserves. The following day, the
Russian diesel tanker is due in Norfolk.

97
00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:33.200
Then on the third of November
come the US midterms,

98
00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:36.960
the stated target date for price
relief from the Russian diesel waiver.

99
00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.480
Further out, Guyana's first Uaru
cargo is due in mid-December,

100
00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:45.840
and the EU ban on long-term Russian
liquefied natural gas imports begins

101
00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:47.520
on New Year's Day.

102
00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.280
That's the brief. Sources for everything
you heard are in the show notes.
